--- context (Desktop 2 / Phoenix, 2026-09-06) ---
Model: models/yolov8x_r1280_cut_xint8.onnx -- yolov8x re-exported at imgsz=1280
(vs. the repo's usual 640) via pipelines/yolov8n/1_export.py --weights
models/yolov8x.pt --size 1280, head-cut via 1b_cut_head.py, quantized plain
XINT8 (no AdaRound) via 3b_quantize_cut.py --calib-dir data/coco_calib --limit 4
(deliberately thin calibration -- this is a throughput/partitioning probe, not
an accuracy claim; do not cite this model's mAP, none was measured).
onnx-tool: 524,093,725,984 MACs/inference (vs. 131,125,732,384 at 640 -- almost
exactly the expected 4x for 4x the pixels).
Achieved TOPS at N=4: 524093725984 * 2 * 6.0 / 1e12 = 6.289 TOPS = 39.31% of the
16 TOPS nameplate -- statistically identical to yolov8l cut XINT8 at 640 split
the same way (6.291 TOPS, 39.32%, results/multi_partition_yolov8l.log), despite
6.2x the MACs per inference. Two very different model/resolution combinations
converging on the same ~39.3% figure is evidence of a real per-column ceiling,
not a coincidence from one lucky config.

Caveats: 1x4.xclbin is deprecated by AMD (Ryzen AI 1.5+, "no longer supported
and should not be used"); this quantized model is throughput/correctness-only,
never evaluated for mAP; memory during quantization was watched closely after
an earlier attempt at --limit 64 exhausted the machine's free RAM (0.44 GB
free before it was killed) -- --limit 4 kept peak calibration cache under
control (recovered to 6-9 GB free during the run, never approached the 4 GB
auto-kill threshold used to supervise it).
